// # ooda/std Standard Library Audit TLDR
//
// Logline: 8-Dimensional Red Team audit of the ooda/std standard library (6605 .oo files across 37 subdirectories). Documents the systemic Pillar 6 mod.oo seam violation, the 256-line cap compliance, the representative crypto/mod.oo fix applied, and the audit verdict for the 35 subdirectories that still lack a mod.oo entry door.
//
// Setup: This is a project documentation file at the strategic governance plane, sibling to openOODA/runtime/SUBSTRATE_AUDIT_TLDR.oot. The audit covers the entire ooda/std/ tree.
//
// Beats:
//   1. Beat 1: Audit methodology and scope.
//   2. Beat 2: 256-line cap compliance verification.
//   3. Beat 3: Pillar 6 mod.oo seam violation inventory (35/37 subdirectories affected at audit time).
//   4. Beat 4: Representative first-principles fix: crypto/mod.oo created.
//   5. Beat 5: Audit verdict and path forward for the remaining 34 subdirectories.
//   6. Beat 6: Batch 1 Pillar 6 closure progress (Sept 1 2026): 5 subdirectories closed (core, math, hash, io, collections). 24 still pending.
//   7. Beat 7: Batch 2 Pillar 6 stub-upgrade progress (Sept 1 2026): 4 stubs upgraded to canonical pattern (ai, phys, simd, text). 24 new mod.oo files still pending.

# === Beat 1: Audit methodology ===

// The audit used the 8-Dimensional Red Team matrix (probes e1 through e8)
// per REDTEAM.oot against the ooda/std/ tree. Scope: 6605 .oo files
// across 37 first-level subdirectories plus 10 root-level .oo files.
// The audit focused on two systemic questions:
//   (1) Is the 256-line cap (per FORMAT.oot §1.3) enforced?
//   (2) Is the Pillar 6 mod.oo seam rule (per FORMAT.oot §1.4) enforced?
//
// Subdirectories surveyed: actor, ai, archive, bio, chem, cli, collections,
// compression, compile, core, crypto, data, diag, econ, encoding, fmt,
// formal_proofs, fs, hash, hw, io, markup, math, net, os, phys, principles,
// proof_verifiers, robot, sec, simd, sync, test, text, time, ui. (35 of 37
// lack mod.oo; only fmt/ and net/ have it.)

# === Beat 2: 256-line cap compliance ===

// The 256-line cap is per-FORMAT.oot §1.3 a project-wide invariant for
// .oo and .oot files. A targeted scan of the largest .oo files in
// ooda/std found:
//
//   std/simd/simd_ops_body.oo         250 lines  (within cap)
//   std/crypto/chacha20.oo            249 lines  (within cap)
//   std/crypto/sha512.oo              231 lines  (within cap)
//   std/crypto/curve25519.oo          244 lines  (within cap)
//   std/crypto/sha256_engine.oo       224 lines  (within cap)
//   std/crypto/keys_engine.oo         226 lines  (within cap)
//
// Conclusion: the .oo files in ooda/std are 256-line compliant. The cap
// invariant holds at the file level. (Earlier suspicion of > 256 lines
// was a size-based false positive -- crypto and SIMD files have very
// long single-line constants, not excess code.)

# === Beat 3: Pillar 6 mod.oo seam violation inventory ===

// Per FORMAT.oot §1.4: "When a subsystem is decomposed into multiple
// sub-256 line .oo files within a directory, the directory MUST contain
// a mod.oo file. mod.oo serves as the single public entry door."
//
// Survey: 35 of 37 first-level subdirectories originally lacked mod.oo.
// Only fmt/ and net/ had it. This is a systemic Pillar 6 violation.
//
//   Subdirectory   mod.oo  .oo files   Status
//   actor          NO      17          violation
//   ai             YES     33          compliant (Batch 2 stub upgrade, Sept 1 2026)
//   archive        NO      21          violation
//   bio            NO      43          violation
//   chem           NO      17          violation
//   cli            NO      17          violation
//   collections    YES     74          compliant (Batch 1, Sept 1 2026)
//   compression    NO      17          violation
//   compile        NO      11          violation
//   core           YES     18          compliant (Batch 1, Sept 1 2026)
//   crypto         YES     61          compliant (FIXED -- see Beat 4)
//   data           NO      17          violation
//   diag           NO      33          violation
//   econ           NO      20          violation
//   encoding       NO      37          violation
//   formal_proofs  NO       8          violation
//   fs             NO      22          violation
//   hash           YES     41          compliant (Batch 1, Sept 1 2026)
//   hw             NO      14          violation
//   io             YES     39          compliant (Batch 1, Sept 1 2026)
//   markup         NO      28          violation
//   math           YES     91          compliant (Batch 1, Sept 1 2026)
//   net            YES     71          compliant
//   os             NO      42          violation
//   phys           YES     12          compliant (Batch 2 stub upgrade, Sept 1 2026)
//   principles     NO      23          violation
//   proof_verifiers NO      8          violation
//   robot          NO      54          violation
//   sec            NO      51          violation
//   simd           YES     35          compliant (Batch 2 stub upgrade, Sept 1 2026)
//   sync           NO      52          violation
//   test           NO      17          violation
//   text           YES     54          compliant (Batch 2 stub upgrade, Sept 1 2026)
//   time           NO      12          violation
//   ui             NO      18          violation
//   fmt            YES      5          compliant
//
// 24 of 37 subdirectories still violate Pillar 6. (Was 35 before
// Batch 1+2 on Sept 1 2026; 5 new mod.oo + 4 stub upgrades = 9 closed.)

# === Beat 4: Representative first-principles fix: crypto/mod.oo ===

// The crypto/ subdirectory was chosen as the most security-critical
// representative. It contains 61 .oo files implementing SHA-256/512,
// HMAC, AES, chacha20, chacha20-poly1305, ed25519, curve25519, ECDSA,
// ML-KEM, BLS, Diffie-Hellman, HKDF, PBKDF2, Argon2, Merkle trees, and
// the constant-time primitive surface.
//
// Fix: created ooda/std/crypto/mod.oo as the single public entry door.
// The mod.oo imports the public-API siblings and re-exports them with
// a stable crypto_mod_* prefix to avoid name collisions with the
// private internal siblings (cores, stubs, invariants, theory,
// principle, kat).
//
// Public API surface (11 functions re-exported):
//   crypto_mod_hash_sha256
//   crypto_mod_hash_hmac_sha256
//   crypto_mod_sym_aes_encrypt
//   crypto_mod_sym_aes_decrypt
//   crypto_mod_sym_chacha20_encrypt
//   crypto_mod_sym_chacha20_decrypt
//   crypto_mod_asy_ed25519_sign_hex
//   crypto_mod_asy_ed25519_verify_hex
//   crypto_mod_asy_curve25519_diffie_hellman
//   crypto_mod_kdf_hkdf
//   crypto_mod_kdf_pbkdf2
//
// Private siblings documented (do not import directly):
//   _core files: chacha20_core.oo, blake3_pure_core.oo, poly1305_core.oo
//   _stubs files: fhe_stubs.oo, keys_stubs.oo, attestation_stubs.oo
//   _invariants files: crypto_invariants.oo
//   _theory files: ml_kem_theory.oo, bls_signature_aggregation_law.oo
//   _principle files: constant_time_principle.oo, diffie_hellman_principle.oo
//   _kat files: crypto_kat.oo
//   _inv, _types, _store internal helpers
//
// Empirical verification: ooda/qa/tests_sovereign/tier1_features/test_t1_f22_crypto_mod_seam.oo
//   (15 assertions, 5 beats, 11 surface functions verified) is in the
//   working tree and parses/type-checks under ooda run.
//
// The mod.oo compiles cleanly under ooda check: "OK".

# === Beat 5: Audit verdict and path forward ===

// First-principles design (E-M / 80-20 / blue ocean):
//   Thrust -- the crypto/mod.oo fix closes the Pillar 6 seam violation
//     for the most security-critical subdirectory in one move. 80/20:
//     1 file (~100 lines) closes the violation for 61 internal files.
//   Drag -- none added. mod.oo is pure re-export; no new allocations,
//     no new subshells, no new process invocations.
//   Weight -- net +116 lines (mod.oo) +128 lines (test) = +244 lines
//     for the seam. 34 subdirectories still lack mod.oo.
//   Velocity -- unchanged for external callers (the re-exports are
//     trivial wrapper functions). Internal callers continue to be
//     importable directly; the mod.oo is a new entry point, not a
//     replacement.
//
// Path forward for the 24 remaining subdirectories:
//   1. Identify the 5-15 most-used public functions per subdirectory.
//   2. Create <subdir>/mod.oo with the re-export pattern (see crypto/).
//   3. Add a tier1 seam verification test (see test_t1_f22_).
//   4. Update openOODA/std/AUDIT_TLDR.oot to mark as compliant.
//
// This is a mechanical but large refactor (~340 lines of mod.oo
// + ~150 lines per test, x 34 subdirectories = ~17,000 lines). Out
// of scope for this audit pass; the crypto/ fix is the representative
// proof-of-concept.
//
// Progress: Batch 1 (Sept 1 2026) closed 5 of the 34 subdirectories
// (core, math, hash, io, collections), bringing the remaining count
// to 24 plus 4 stub upgrades (ai, phys, simd, text). See Beat 6.

# === Beat 6: Batch 1 Pillar 6 closure progress (Sept 1 2026) ===

// Five high-leverage subdirectories were closed in Batch 1, following
// the representative crypto/mod.oo pattern. Each new mod.oo re-exports
// a stable <subdir>_mod_* surface for the most-used public functions,
// documents the private sibling inventory, and is verified by a tier1
// seam test (test_t1_f22_<subdir>_mod_seam.oo) with 15+ assertions
// and 5 beats covering type-stability, prefix-stability, and triple-run
// referential transparency.
//
// Batch 1 deliverables (10 files, 976 lines aggregate, 80 re-exports):
//   core/mod.oo         68 lines / 24 re-exports   | hash/mod.oo        69 lines / 15
//   math/mod.oo         58 lines / 15 re-exports   | io/mod.oo          59 lines / 10
//   collections/mod.oo  62 lines / 16 re-exports   |
//   test_t1_f22_core_mod_seam.oo        142 lines  | test_t1_f22_hash_mod_seam.oo       132 lines
//   test_t1_f22_math_mod_seam.oo        130 lines  | test_t1_f22_io_mod_seam.oo         123 lines
//   test_t1_f22_collections_mod_seam.oo  133 lines  |
//
// Surface categories (one line per subdirectory):
//   core_mod_*        : Option 5, Result 5, List 4, Str 4, JSON 2, DomainCert 2, Hash 2
//   math_mod_*        : Stats 4, BigInt 2, Complex 4, Matrix 2, Constants 3
//   hash_mod_*        : SHA-2 2, SHA-3 2, Keccak 2, BLAKE3 1, Legacy 2, NonCrypto 6
//   io_mod_*          : Reader 2, Writer 2, Cursor 1, MemStream 2, LineReader 1, Bit 2
//   collections_mod_* : Catalog 2, Vec 3, Queue 3, Stack 3, Deque 1, HashMap 3, HashSet 1
//
// Verification: each test asserts 15-19 invariants (T1.F22.1 through
// T1.F22.19), including surface-length, prefix-stability for every
// documented name, and triple-run referential transparency.
//
// Remaining work:
//   - Batches 3-7: close the remaining 24 subdirectories: actor,
//     archive, bio, chem, cli, compile, compression, data, diag, econ,
//     encoding, formal_proofs, fs, hw, markup, os, principles,
//     proof_verifiers, robot, sec, sync, test, time, ui.

# === Beat 7: Batch 2 Pillar 6 stub-upgrade progress (Sept 1 2026) ===

// Four stub mod.oo files (ai, phys, simd, text) were upgraded to the
// canonical crypto/mod.oo pattern: 4-element Academy header, imports
// of public-API siblings, re-export wrappers with <subdir>_mod_*
// prefix, and a private-sibling inventory documenting what NOT to
// import. Each upgrade is verified by a tier1 seam test with 17-26
// assertions and 5 beats.
//
// Batch 2 deliverables (8 files, 984 lines aggregate, 123 re-exports):
//   ai/mod.oo       75 lines / 22 re-exports    | simd/mod.oo      99 lines / 35
//   phys/mod.oo     63 lines / 18 re-exports    | text/mod.oo     135 lines / 48
//   test_t1_f22_ai_mod_seam.oo    137 lines     | test_t1_f22_simd_mod_seam.oo  159 lines
//   test_t1_f22_phys_mod_seam.oo  137 lines     | test_t1_f22_text_mod_seam.oo  179 lines
//
// Surface categories (one line per domain):
//   ai_mod_*      : Tensor 8, Transformer 2, Quant 3, GGUF 3, Synth/Repair/Pair/Evolution 4, Scaling 2
//   phys_mod_*    : Thermo 2, Conservation 2, Action 2, Gravity 2, Hawking 2, Berry 3, SYK 2, Law 3
//   simd_mod_*    : Vec8f 6, Vec16f 5, Vec4f 4, Vec4i 2, Blend 2, Clamp 2, Gather 1, Pack 2, Quat 5, Noise 3, Shuffle 3
//   text_mod_*    : Regex 6, KMP 3, Levenshtein 4, Hamming/Jaro 2, Format 5, StringBuilder 4, Rope 4, UTF-8/Grapheme 4, Unicode 4, Diff 3, Case/Slug/Sort 5, Hex/Count/Wrap 4
//
// Breaking-change note: the previous ai/mod.oo used a non-canonical
// ai_tensor_* prefix on its 7 re-exports. Batch 2 migrates those and
// adds 15 more to use the canonical ai_mod_* prefix. External callers
// referencing ai_tensor_* must update to ai_mod_tensor_*.
//
// Pillar 6 compliance after Batch 2: 12 of 37 subdirectories are fully
// compliant (3 original + 5 from Batch 1 + 4 stubs upgraded in Batch 2).
// 24 of 37 still need new mod.oo files.
//
// Residual structural concerns:
//   A. Cross-subdirectory imports: a few internal siblings are
//      imported by external callers via "crypto/sha256.oo" etc.
//      Pillar 6 says these should be "crypto/mod.oo". Migration is
//      non-breaking (mod.oo coexists with sibling files) but should
//      be enforced via the enforcer in a future pass.
//   B. Root-level .oo files (byte.oo, math.oo, option.oo, etc.) are
//      10 standalone .oo files at ooda/std/ root. They predate the
//      mod.oo rule and serve a similar purpose for primitives. They
//      should be migrated to ooda/std/core/mod.oo or similar.
//   C. The audit was a static review. No dynamic analysis (e.g.,
//      "which private siblings are actually imported by external
//      callers") was performed. Recommended for a follow-up audit.

# === Audit verdict ===

// 256-line compliant at the file level. After Batches 1+2 (Sept 1 2026),
// 12 of 37 first-level subdirectories have a full mod.oo seam (3 original:
// fmt, net, crypto + 5 from Batch 1: core, math, hash, io, collections +
// 4 stubs upgraded in Batch 2: ai, phys, simd, text). 24 of 37 still need
// the full re-export pattern.
